# ==========================================================================
#  O38  Rung four, domain and range: inference rules, not constraints
# ==========================================================================
#
#  DECIDES
#    A domain is declared only when everything that will ever have the
#    property is of that class, because the reasoner will type whatever has
#    it; the Trail's bs:locatedIn gets a union domain for that reason.
#
#  WHY
#    Every newcomer reads rdfs:domain as a constraint: 'only bookshops may
#    have a location'. RDF Semantics reads it as a rule: 'whatever has a
#    location is a bookshop'. The two agree on clean data and part company
#    on the first publisher with an address. Declare bs:locatedIn's domain
#    as bs:Bookshop, load the people file, and the reasoner types Northwind
#    Press as a bookshop, without error or warning. This lesson's file does
#    that, and the closure measured on it contains the triple.
#
#    Range is the same rule in the other direction: whatever is the value
#    of bs:locatedIn is a Settlement. That one is usually wanted, and it
#    still bites: a range of xsd:integer on a property whose values are
#    strings does not reject the strings, it declares them integers.
#
#    The habit that follows is the one from o31. Declare a domain only when
#    every use will agree with it, and when two classes will share a
#    property, declare the union, which is the next rung's vocabulary. Or
#    declare nothing, as gist mostly does, and let the shapes say what the
#    data must look like. The retyping is not a bug in RDFS. It is RDFS
#    doing exactly what it says, and the SHACL course's s59, the inference
#    that hides an error, is the same fact seen from the validator's side.
#
#  DIAGRAM
#       rdfs:domain, read as a constraint (wrong)       rdfs:domain, read as a rule (right)
#
#       only Bookshops may be locatedIn                 whatever is locatedIn is a Bookshop
#       Northwind Press locatedIn Edinburgh => error    Northwind Press locatedIn Edinburgh
#                                                         => Northwind Press a Bookshop   (no error, no warning)
#
#       the rule (rdfs2):  p domain C . x p y  =>  x type C
#
#       the fix: a union domain, or none, and a shape for the constraint
#
#  WHAT TO TAKE AWAY
#    - rdfs:domain and rdfs:range add types. They never reject anything.
#    - Declare a domain only when every use of the property will agree
#      with it. Two classes sharing a property want a union or nothing.
#    - The constraint you meant is a shape. The SHACL course's s59 shows
#      the other half of this lesson.
#
#  TRY IT
#    Show Facts. Northwind Press is now a bs:Bookshop. Change the domain to
#    a union of Bookshop and Publisher, press Get All, Show Facts again:
#    the union is a class with no name, and Northwind is typed with that
#    instead, which is true and useless. Remove the domain.
#
#  CORNER    referent
#  RUNS IN   the browser
#  LOAD IT   https://semantechs.co.uk/turtle-editor-viewer/?dot=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fpwin%2FOntology_Course%2Fmain%2Flessons%2F06-the-ladder%2Fo38-rung-four-domain-and-range-inference-rules-not-constraints.ttl
#  SOURCES   RDF Schema 3.2 rdfs:domain
#            https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf12-schema/#ch_domain
#            RDF Schema 3.1 rdfs:range
#            https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf12-schema/#ch_range
#  MEASURED  parses, 21 triples; 3 classes, 1 object property, 2 individuals; OWL 2 DL in profile; RL closure +3; 'Every bookshop, after the cl' 2 rows
# ==========================================================================

@prefix bt:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/> .
@prefix bs:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

skos:prefLabel      a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:altLabel       a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:definition     a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:scopeNote      a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:editorialNote  a owl:AnnotationProperty .

bs:Bookshop    a owl:Class ; skos:prefLabel "Bookshop"@en .
bs:Publisher   a owl:Class ; skos:prefLabel "Publisher"@en .
bs:Settlement  a owl:Class ; skos:prefLabel "Settlement"@en .

# The narrow domain, declared to show what it does.
bs:locatedIn
    a            owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain  bs:Bookshop ;
    rdfs:range   bs:Settlement ;
    skos:editorialNote "Domain too narrow, on purpose: the reasoner will type every publisher with an address as a bookshop."@en .

bt:shop-inkwell   a bs:Bookshop ;  bs:locatedIn bt:place-wigtown .
bt:pub-northwind  a bs:Publisher ; bs:locatedIn bt:place-edinburgh .
bt:place-wigtown    skos:prefLabel "Wigtown"@en .
bt:place-edinburgh  skos:prefLabel "Edinburgh"@en .


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  AFTERWARDS.  Queries to run in the SPARQL panel, on this file (press
#  Add Prefixes first) or on the data file it names.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#  -- Every bookshop, after the closure  (on the reasoned)
#  PREFIX bs: <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#>
#  SELECT ?shop WHERE { ?shop a bs:Bookshop }
